.TH ARES_SET_DNSSEC_CRYPTO_FUNCTIONS 3 "28 September 2026"
.SH NAME
ares_set_dnssec_crypto_functions \- Set the cryptographic functions for DNSSEC
validation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ares.h>

struct ares_dnssec_crypto_functions {
  unsigned int version;

  ares_bool_t (*alg_supported)(unsigned char algorithm, void *user_data);

  ares_status_t (*verify)(unsigned char algorithm, const unsigned char *key,
                          size_t key_len, const unsigned char *data,
                          size_t data_len, const unsigned char *sig,
                          size_t sig_len, void *user_data);

  ares_bool_t (*digest_supported)(unsigned char digest_type, void *user_data);

  ares_status_t (*digest)(unsigned char digest_type, const unsigned char *data,
                          size_t data_len, unsigned char *out,
                          size_t *out_len, void *user_data);
};

ares_status_t ares_set_dnssec_crypto_functions(
  ares_channel_t *channel, const struct ares_dnssec_crypto_functions *funcs,
  void *user_data);
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
c-ares has no cryptography of its own.  DNSSEC validation with
\fBares_query_dnssec(3)\fP verifies signatures and calculates hashes with
functions the application provides, usually from the TLS library it uses.

The \fBares_set_dnssec_crypto_functions(3)\fP function sets them for the
channel
.IR channel .
The function table
.I funcs
is copied, and
.I user_data
is passed to each function.  When
.I funcs
is NULL, the functions are removed and \fBares_query_dnssec(3)\fP fails with
.BR ARES_ENOTIMP .
The functions are copied to channels made with \fBares_dup(3)\fP.

The functions are called with the channel lock held, from the thread that
processes the channel.  They must not call c-ares functions on the channel.

All members of the table are required:
.TP 18
.B version
The version of the structure, must be 1.
.TP 18
.B alg_supported
Return
.B ARES_TRUE
if signatures with the DNSSEC algorithm number
.I algorithm
can be verified.
.TP 18
.B verify
Verify the signature
.I sig
of the data
.I data
with the public key
.IR key .
The data is hashed as the algorithm requires.  Return
.B ARES_SUCCESS
if the signature is valid,
.B ARES_ENOMEM
when out of memory, and anything else (like
.BR ARES_EBADRESP )
if it is not.
.TP 18
.B digest_supported
Return
.B ARES_TRUE
if the digest type
.I digest_type
can be calculated.
.TP 18
.B digest
Hash
.I data
with the digest type
.IR digest_type ,
writing the digest to
.IR out .
.I out_len
is the size of
.I out
(at least 48), and is set to the length of the digest.  Return
.B ARES_SUCCESS
or an error like
.BR ARES_ENOMEM .
.PP

The keys and signatures are in the formats DNSSEC uses.  c-ares checks them
before the functions are called: RSA keys of 512 to 4096 bits (1024 to 4096 for
RSASHA512) with exponents of at most 8 octets, and the lengths of the keys and
signatures of the other algorithms.  The algorithms, by DNSSEC algorithm number
(RFC 8624), are:
.TP 12
.B 5, 7, 8, 10
RSASHA1, RSASHA1-NSEC3-SHA1, RSASHA256 and RSASHA512: RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 with
SHA-1, SHA-1, SHA-256 and SHA-512.  The key is in the format of RFC 3110 section
2: the exponent length in one octet, or a zero octet and the length in two
octets, followed by the exponent and the modulus, all big-endian.  The signature
is as long as the modulus.
.TP 12
.B 13, 14
ECDSAP256SHA256 and ECDSAP384SHA384: ECDSA with P-256 and SHA-256, and with
P-384 and SHA-384.  The key is the point as x | y (64 or 96 octets), the
signature is r | s (64 or 96 octets), as in RFC 6605 section 4.
.TP 12
.B 15, 16
ED25519 and ED448: PureEdDSA (RFC 8080), with a key of 32 or 57 octets and a
signature of 64 or 114 octets.
.PP
The digest types, as in DS records, are 1 (SHA-1), 2 (SHA-256) and 4
(SHA-384).

Every validator must support the algorithms 8 and 13 and the digest types 1
and 2 (RFC 8624).  Signatures made with an algorithm that is not supported are
treated like no signatures, so a zone signed only with such algorithms is
validated as insecure (RFC 4035 section 5.2).  Signatures with the SHA-1 based
algorithms 5 and 7 are never accepted (RFC 9905), whatever
.B alg_supported
returns.

.SH RETURN VALUES
\fBares_set_dnssec_crypto_functions(3)\fP returns
.B ARES_SUCCESS
when the functions were set or removed, and
.B ARES_EFORMERR
when
.I channel
is NULL, the version is not 1, a function is missing, or the functions do not
support the algorithms 8 and 13 and the digest types 1 and 2.

.SH AVAILABILITY
\fBares_set_dnssec_crypto_functions(3)\fP was introduced in c-ares 1.35.0.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ares_query_dnssec (3),
.BR ares_dup (3)
